'''tup-man''' (tup′man), ''n.'' A breeder of or dealer in tups. [Local, Eng.]

'''tupsee''' (tup′sē), ''n.'' The mango-fish, ''Polynemus paradoxus.''

'''tuque''' (tūk), ''n.'' [Canadian F. form of F. ''toque'', a cap: see ''toque.''] A cap worn in Canada. See the quotation.
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Tuque.<br>''a'', folded to fit the head; ''b'', as knitted.}}}}
{{smaller block|But the ''tuque'' is disappearing, we are sorry to say, and ordinary caps are taking its place. It alone served to mark the habitant. It is something like a long stocking, knit and closed at both ends, and one end being pushed into the other to double it, it is drawn over the head, down the back of the neck, and indeed over the whole face and shoulders if necessary. . . . The sash . . . has been adopted as an ornamental and useful appendage by the citizens; and the snow-shoe clubs have adopted the ''tuque.''
{{right|''The Century'', II. 454.}}}}

