bant'', F. ''turban'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''turbante;'' also in a more orig. form, early mod. E. ''tuliban, tolliban, tulibant, tolibant, tulipant, tolipant, tolipane'' = D. ''tulband, tulpe'', < OF. ''toliban, tollipan, tolopan'' (ML. ''tulipantus'', also ''tulipa''); < Turk. ''tulbend, dulbend'' = Ar. ''dulband'', < Pers. Hind. ''dulband'', a turban. From the same source is E. ''tulip'', lit. ‘a turban’: see ''tulip''.] '''1.''' The distinctive head-dress of men of the Moslem nations, consisting of a scarf or shawl wound around the tarboosh. {{smaller|The color and material of the scarf differ with the rank and position of the wearer,}}
