{{smaller block|They wrappe and fold together . . . almost as much linnen upon their heads as the Turks doe in those linnen caps they weare, which are called ''Turbents.''
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 90.}}}}
'''2.''' A modification of the Oriental turban, worn by women in Europe and America during the first half of the nineteenth century.
{{smaller block|I was anxious to prevent her from disfiguring her small gentle mousey face with a great Saracen’s-head ''turban.''
{{right|''Mrs. Gaskell'', Cranford, ix.}}}}
'''3.''' A head-dress consisting of a bright-colored handkerchief or square of cotton, worn by negro women in the West Indies and the southern United States.
{{smaller block|A black woman in blue cotton gown, red-and-yellow Madras ''turban'', . . . crouched against the wall.
{{right|''G. W. Cable'', Au Large, i.}}}}
